un, there simply aren't as many pass attempts to go around. He's a safer floor than ceiling play in this spot.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Tucker Kraft</strong> (TE, 6 targets, 52 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>ATL \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>ATL are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>33%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 ATL project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Bijan Robinson</strong>'s rushing projection (15 carries, 73 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Drake London</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, ATL funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>8 targets for 60 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Kyle Pitts</strong> (TE, 7 targets, 59 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 8-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the 